5. Linguistic legitimacy and symbolic power:
   Bourdieu

   How it mirrors our case:
   Our case shows language being treated as a status signal. Forum-native style, academic tone, and familiar rhetorical form are used as gates for legitimacy. This matters because good word use does not imply good person, good intent, or good analysis. Polished language can exclude. Awkward language can still carry the object.

   Ref:
   Pierre Bourdieu.
   Language and Symbolic Power.
   Edited by John B. Thompson.
   Translated by Gino Raymond and Matthew Adamson.
   Polity Press, 1991.
